    "result_text": "I added `login_required` and applied it to the views that need it. I couldn't run the import check or the tests because the shell command needed approval, so none of this has been run.\n\n**The decorator** is in `CTFd/utils/decorators/__init__.py`. It's built from the catalog pattern `decorators.authed_only@v1`, with the `# pcat:` marker kept above it. `check_code` reported no deviations from the pattern. It does three things:\n- Authenticated users go through to the view.\n- JSON and event-stream requests get a 403.\n- Everyone else is redirected to `auth.login` with `next=<current path>`.\n\n**Where it's applied:**\n- `CTFd/views.py`: `settings`\n- `CTFd/users.py`: `private` (`/profile`, `/user`)\n- `CTFd/teams.py`: `join`, `new`, `private`\n- `CTFd/events/__init__.py`: `subscribe` (above `@ratelimit`)\n\n**Existing code was already broken.** These modules already imported `authed_only` from the decorators module, but nothing defined it, so they would fail on import. Besides the views above, the `CTFd/api/v1/` modules (`shares`, `tokens`, `teams`, `unlocks`, `solutions`, `challenges`, `users`) import it too. I didn't edit those about 30 call sites. Instead I added `authed_only = login_required` as an alias so they resolve. If you'd rather rename them all, that's a mechanical find-and-replace.\n\nPlease run `python -c \"import CTFd.views, CTFd.teams, CTFd.users, CTFd.events, CTFd.api.v1.users\"` and the test suite to confirm the imports now resolve."
  },
